        <div id="matching" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How to Match a Blade to Your Mower
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before choosing a blade type or brand, you need to know exactly what fits. The easiest method is the part number. Most blades have one stamped into the steel, and your owner's manual lists the correct part for your model. Search that number and most aftermarket makers will show their cross-referenced equivalents. If the stamp has worn off, use your mower's model number, which is on a label on the deck or frame.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have neither, measure the old blade. Take it off and measure its length straight from one end to the other. Do not assume that a 21-inch mower takes a 21-inch blade, because many do not, and riding mower deck widths do not divide neatly by the number of blades either. Measure the width of the blade and, if you can, the thickness.
          </p>

          <p>
            The center hole is where most people get caught out. Blades attach with a round hole, a star with five or six points, a bow-tie, a hex, or a combination of a center hole and extra mounting holes. The shape has to match the adapter or spindle on your mower exactly. A star-hole blade on a round spindle will not seat properly, and an unseated blade can come loose. Measure the hole diameter and note its shape before you buy.
          </p>

