        <div id="blade-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. High-Lift vs Mulching vs Low-Lift vs Gator
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once you know what fits, choose a blade type. The difference is in the lift, the raised sail at the back of each cutting end. As the blade spins, the sail pulls air upward, which stands grass up before it is cut and then pushes clippings out of the chute or into the bag.
          </p>

          <p>
            High-lift blades have a tall, steep sail. They create the strongest suction, which gives the cleanest cut on tall or dense grass and fills a bag tightly. The cost is power. High-lift blades need more horsepower, and in sandy or dusty soil they suck grit into the deck, which wears both the blade and the deck faster. Blade makers broadly class high-lift as roughly 1 inch to 1-3/16 inches of sail height, and low-lift as under 3/4 inch.
          </p>

          <p>
            Low-lift blades have a shallow sail. They need less power, make less noise and throw up less dust, which makes them the right choice for sandy soil, dry conditions and low cutting heights. They do not bag as well. Medium-lift blades, which most mowers come with as standard, sit between the two.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mulching blades have a curved profile with extra cutting surfaces that recut clippings into fine pieces before they fall back into the lawn. They work best on mowers with a mulching deck or mulch plug, and on lawns mowed often. Gator-style or 3-in-1 blades combine a mulching profile with teeth that recirculate clippings and enough lift to bag or discharge as well, which makes them a good all-round choice for mowers used in more than one mode.
          </p>

