        <div id="by-mower" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Blade for Which Mower
          </h2>

          <p>
            Walk-behind push and self-propelled mowers are the easiest case. Most are sold as 3-in-1 mowers that bag, mulch and discharge, so a Gator-style or 3-in-1 mulching blade suits them best. If you mostly bag, a high-lift blade will fill the bag more tightly; if you mostly mulch, a dedicated mulching blade gives the finest finish. For help choosing the mower itself, our guide to the <a href="/best-self-propelled-lawn-mower/">best self-propelled lawn mowers</a> compares deck designs as well as engines.
          </p>

          <p>
            Riding mowers and lawn tractors usually have two or three blades on a wider deck and a stronger engine, so high-lift blades are easier to run. They also cover enough ground that edge life starts to matter, which is where a carbide-edged blade like the Oregon G5 earns its price. If you are still choosing a tractor, see our picks for the <a href="/best-riding-lawn-mower/">best riding lawn mowers</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Zero-turn mowers are the most demanding. They move fast, often over rough ground, and their decks are designed for heavy blades and high airflow. A thick, commercial-grade blade like the Oregon G6 resists bending and keeps its edge across acres, and high-lift or Gator blades suit the speeds involved. If you are shopping for one, our guide to the <a href="/best-zero-turn-mower/">best zero-turn mowers</a> covers deck construction in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Reel mowers are the exception to all of this. They do not use a flat rotary blade at all; they have a spinning cylinder of blades that shears grass against a fixed bedknife. They are maintained by adjustment and backlapping rather than blade replacement. If you are considering one for a small lawn, our guide to the <a href="/best-reel-mower/">best reel mowers</a> explains which grasses and yards they suit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="oem-vs-aftermarket" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. OEM vs Aftermarket
          </h2>

          <p>
            OEM blades come from the company that made your mower, or its parts supplier. They are designed with the deck, guaranteed to fit when ordered by model and serial number, and the safest choice on a mower still under warranty. They are also usually the most expensive, and most decks are offered in only one or two blade styles.
          </p>

          <p>
            Good aftermarket blades from established makers like Oregon, Rotary and 8TEN are built to OEM dimensions and cross-referenced by OEM part number. Many are made to the same standards and some to higher ones. They also offer much more choice of type: a low-lift blade for sandy ground, a thicker blade for a deck that keeps bending the stock one, or a carbide edge for longer life.
          </p>

          <p>
            The risk with aftermarket blades is at the bottom of the market. Unbranded multipacks are sometimes excellent and sometimes soft, badly balanced or slightly the wrong size. A blade that is too soft will dull and bend quickly; a blade that is too hard and brittle can crack. We would rather pay a little more for a known maker than take the chance.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">OUR RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Newer mower or unusual deck: buy OEM. Everything else: buy a quality aftermarket line.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Twin-blade systems, proprietary adapters and in-warranty mowers are worth the OEM price. For standard single- and multi-blade decks, a quality aftermarket blade matched by part number gives you the same fit with more choice.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Whichever you choose, check the listing states that the blade replaces your specific OEM part number, not just that it fits your brand. Brands use different blades across models and years, and a blade for one Toro or Husqvarna may not fit another.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="steel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Hardened Steel, Thickness and Coatings
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mower blades have to be hard enough to hold an edge and tough enough to bend rather than shatter when they hit a rock. That balance comes from the steel and the heat treatment. Most quality blades are made from carbon or high-carbon steel that is hardened and then tempered to reduce brittleness, and reputable makers test hardness on the Rockwell scale as part of quality control.
          </p>

          <p>
            Harder is not automatically better. A very hard blade holds its edge longer but can chip or crack on impact, and a cracked blade is dangerous. A softer blade dulls faster but bends rather than breaks. The good lines aim for a balance, and this is one of the main differences between a quality blade and a cheap one, even when they look identical.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thickness matters for the same reason. A thicker blade resists bending when it strikes something, which keeps it balanced and protects the spindle. Residential blades are relatively thin; commercial blades like the Oregon G6, at 1/4 inch thick, are much heavier. The extra weight is fine on a zero-turn built for it, but it adds load on a small engine, so do not fit a heavier blade than your mower is specified for.
          </p>

          <p>
            Edge treatments are the newest development. Oregon's Fusion edge on the G5 and G6 bonds a layer of tungsten carbide along the cutting edge. It is much harder than steel, so it keeps the edge sharp longer, while the body of the blade stays tough enough to absorb impacts. For mowers that cover a lot of ground, that is a real advantage. Some blades are also painted or coated to resist rust, which helps a mower stored in a damp shed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="balancing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Balancing a Blade
          </h2>

          <p>
            An unbalanced blade vibrates, and that vibration wears out the spindle bearings, loosens bolts and, on a walk-behind mower, makes the handle buzz in your hands. It is one of the most common causes of early spindle failure. Checking balance takes seconds and should be done every time a blade is fitted or sharpened.
          </p>

          <p>
            The simplest method is to hang the blade on a nail through its center hole and see whether one end drops. A better method is a cone balancer, an inexpensive tool that the blade sits on. If one side drops, that side is heavier; grind a little metal off the back of that end, not from the cutting edge, and check again until the blade sits level.
          </p>

          <p>
            New blades from good makers are usually balanced out of the box, but it is still worth checking. Budget blades vary more. Sharpened blades need checking every time, because sharpening removes metal unevenly unless you are careful to take the same amount off each end.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">BLADE CARE AT A GLANCE</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Every time</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Check balance after sharpening</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">All at once</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Replace every blade on a deck together</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Sail up</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Fitted the right way round</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Spark plug</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Disconnect before touching a blade</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If a blade is bent, do not try to balance or straighten it. A bent blade is weakened and can crack. Replace it, and check the spindle and adapter for damage at the same time, since whatever bent the blade may have bent those too.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="installing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Installing a Blade Safely
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start by making the mower safe. On a gas mower, disconnect the spark plug wire. On a battery mower, remove the battery. On a riding mower, remove the key and, ideally, disconnect the battery. Never rely on the engine being off; turning a blade by hand can pull a gas engine through a compression stroke and start it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tip a walk-behind mower on its side with the air filter and carburetor facing up, so oil and fuel do not run into them. Wedge a block of wood between the blade and the deck to stop it turning while you loosen the bolt, and wear heavy gloves. On riding mowers, many owners find it easier to remove the deck; check your manual for the method.
          </p>

          <p>
            Note which way round the old blade was fitted before taking it off. The sail, the raised back edge, points up toward the deck. A blade fitted upside down will barely cut and can damage the lawn. Take a photo of the blade, adapter and washers so you can reassemble them in the same order.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tighten the blade bolt to the torque figure in your owner's manual, using a torque wrench if you have one. Too loose and the blade can shift or come off; too tight and you can strip threads or crack an adapter. Many blade bolts are designed to be replaced rather than reused, so check your manual. Finally, reconnect the power and run the mower briefly to check for vibration before mowing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sharpen-or-replace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Sharpen or Replace?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A blade that is simply dull should be sharpened, not replaced. Signs include torn, frayed grass tips that turn brown a day or two after mowing, and a mower that seems to struggle more than it used to. Most home lawns need a sharpen every 20 to 25 hours of mowing, which for a typical suburban lawn works out at two or three times a season. Our guide on <a href="/how-often-to-sharpen-mower-blades/">how often to sharpen mower blades</a> explains the signs and schedule in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Replace a blade when it is bent, cracked, has deep nicks that would take a lot of grinding to remove, or has been sharpened so many times that the blade is noticeably narrower or the sail is worn thin. Sand wears the sail away over time, and a worn sail loses lift, so a blade can still be sharp and no longer bag or discharge well.
          </p>

          <p>
            The best habit is to keep two sets. Fit one, keep a sharp spare set on the shelf, and swap them when the first set dulls. Then sharpen the dull set at your leisure. Swapping blades takes a few minutes, so this makes it easy to always mow with a sharp edge.
          </p>

          <p>
            Carbide-edged blades like the Oregon G5 and G6 need fewer sharpenings, but take more care when you do. Use a file or light passes on a grinder, follow the original angle, and do not overheat the edge. And whatever blade you use, check balance afterward.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Matching by length alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Two 21-inch blades can have completely different center holes, and a blade that does not seat properly on the spindle can come loose. Always match the part number, or measure the length, center hole shape and diameter, and any extra mounting holes before buying. Check the listing names your specific OEM part number.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">High-lift blades on sandy ground</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                High-lift blades pull a lot of air through the deck, and in sandy or dusty yards that air carries grit that sandblasts the blade and deck. The sail wears away and lift drops within a season. On sandy soil, use a low-lift or medium-lift blade and accept slightly less bagging performance.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Fitting the blade upside down</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The raised sail at the back of each cutting edge must point up toward the deck. Fitted upside down, a blade barely cuts, scalps the lawn and throws clippings badly. Photograph the blade, adapter and washers before removing them, so you can reassemble everything the same way round and in the same order.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the balance check</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An unbalanced blade vibrates, wearing spindle bearings and loosening bolts. New budget blades and freshly sharpened blades are the most likely to be out of balance. Hang the blade on a nail or a cone balancer, and grind the heavy end until it sits level. It takes seconds and saves a spindle.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing old and new blades</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                On riding mowers and zero-turns, replacing one blade and leaving the others creates an uneven cut, because the new blade cuts cleanly and the old ones tear. It can also upset the deck's airflow. Replace every blade on a deck at the same time, and keep them the same type and brand.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are mulching blades better than regular blades?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are better for mulching, not for everything. A mulching blade recuts clippings into fine pieces that disappear into the lawn, which works best on a mulching deck mowed often. On long or wet grass, mulching blades clump and struggle. If you switch between bagging, mulching and discharge, a Gator-style or 3-in-1 blade is the best compromise.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a universal blade fit my mower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if it matches your mower's length, center hole shape and diameter, and mounting holes. So-called universal blades come with adapters for several hole shapes, but they are not truly universal, and an adapter that does not seat perfectly is a safety risk. We would rather buy a blade listed for your specific OEM part number.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Oregon Gator blades worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, yes. Gator blades mulch better than most stock blades while still bagging and discharging well, and the G5 and G6 add a carbide edge that stays sharp much longer. On a small lawn mowed a few times a month, the cheaper G3 or a plain replacement blade may be all you need. On bigger lawns, the G5 pays off.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I put a high-lift blade on any mower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if a high-lift version is made for your mower, and ideally only if your engine has power to spare. High-lift blades need more horsepower, so on a small engine they can bog down in thick grass. Avoid them on sandy or dusty ground, where they pull grit into the deck and wear out faster.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I find my mower blade part number?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Look for a number stamped into the blade itself, usually near the center. If it has worn off, check your owner's manual, or find your mower's model number on a label on the deck or frame and search the maker's parts site. Most aftermarket makers let you search by either the OEM part number or the mower model.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do lawn mower blades last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on lawn size, soil and how often you hit things. A blade on a small, clean lawn may last several seasons with regular sharpening; one on a sandy or rocky yard may need replacing every season. Replace blades that are bent, cracked, deeply nicked or worn narrow, even if they are still sharp.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy OEM or aftermarket blades?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Buy OEM for a newer mower under warranty, or for mowers with unusual blade systems, such as twin-blade walk-behinds. For most other mowers, a quality aftermarket blade from Oregon, Rotary or 8TEN, matched by OEM part number, fits the same way, often costs less, and gives you more choice of lift style and edge type.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

